/*!
@defgroup linflexd_lin LINFlexD LIN Driver （Linflexd_lin）
@ingroup linflexd_driver
@brief This section describes the programming interface of the Peripheral driver for LINFlexD in LIN mode.

# LIN Driver Overview {#LINDRIVEROvw}

The LIN (Local Interconnect Network) Driver is an use-case driven High Level Peripheral Driver. The driver provides
users important key features. YUNTU provides LIN Stack as a middleware software package that is developed on LIN driver.
Users also can create their own LIN applications and LIN stack that are compatible with LIN Specification.<br />

# LIN Driver Device structures {#LINDRIVERDevS}

The driver uses instantiations of the linflexd_state_t to maintain the current state of a particular LINFlexD Hardware instance
module driver.<br />
The user is required to provide memory for the driver state structures during the initialization. The driver itself
does not statically allocate memory.

# Integration guideline #

## Compilation units ##

The following files need to be compiled in the project:
\verbatim
${SDK_PATH}\platform\drivers\src\linflexd\linflexd_lin_driver.c
${SDK_PATH}\platform\drivers\src\linflexd\linflexd_lin_irq.c
\endverbatim

## Include path ##

The following paths need to be added to the include path of the toolchain:
\verbatim
${SDK_PATH}\platform\drivers\inc\
\endverbatim

## Compile symbols ##

No special symbols are required for this component

## Dependencies ##

- \ref clock_manager @n
- \ref interrupt_manager @n
- \ref osif @n

# LIN Driver Initialization {#LINDRIVERInit}

1. To initialize the LIN driver, call the LINFlexD_DRV_Init() function and pass the instance number of the relevant LINFlexD
hardware interface instance which is LINFlexD instance in this release.<br />
For example: to use LINFlexD0 pass value 0 to the initialization function.
2. Pass a user configuration structure #linflexd_user_config_t as shown here:
~~~~~{.c}
/* LINFlexD Driver configuration structure */
typedef struct
{
    uint32_t baudRate;                              /*!< baudrate of LIN Hardware Interface to configure */
    bool nodeFunction;                              /*!< Node function as Master or Slave */
    linflexd_break_length_t breakLength;            /*!< Break length, only for master mode */
    bool autobaudEnable;                            /*!< Enable Autobaud feature, only for slave mode */
    bool timeoutEnable;                             /*!< Enable Timeout feature */
    uint8_t responseTimeoutValue;                   /*!< Timeout value for response */
    uint8_t headerTimeoutValue;                     /*!< Timeout value for header */
    uint8_t filterCount;                             /*!< Actual filters used */
    linflexd_id_filter_config_t *slaveFilterCfgPtr; /*!< Slave filter configurations, only for slave mode */
} linflexd_user_config_t;
~~~~~
3. For LINFlexD, typically the user configures the #linflexd_user_config_t instantiation with a baudrate from 1000bps to 20000bps.<br />
-E.g. 19200 bps linUserConfig.baudRate = 19200U.
4. Node function can be MASTER or SLAVE.<br />
-E.g. linUserConfig.nodeFunction = MASTER
5. For LINFlexD only in master mode, typically the user configures the #linflexd_user_config_t instantiation with a breakLength from 10 bits to 50 bits.<br />
-E.g. linUserConfig.breakLength = LINFlexD_BREAK_13_BIT
6. If users do not want to use Autobaud feature, then just configure linUserConfig.autobaudEnable = FALSE.
7. If users do not want to use timeout feature, then just configure linUserConfig.timeoutEnable = FALSE.
8. If timeoutEnable is true, responseTimeoutValue which configures timeout value for response can take effect.
9. If timeoutEnable is true, headerTimeoutValue which configures timeout value for header can take effect.
10. For LINFlexD only in slave mode, typically the user configures the number of actual filters used.
11. User can config several id filter which will be used in slave mode for LINFlexD. This is a code example to configure id filter:
~~~~~{.c}
    linflexd_id_filter_config_t idFilterConfig[1] = 
        {
            {
                .filterEnable = true,
                .maskEnable = false,
                .dataFieldLength = 8,
                .direction = LINFlexD_TX,
                .checksumType = LIN_ENHANCED_CHECKSUM,
                .id = 0x32,
                .data = {0x01, 0x02, 0x03, 0x04, 0x05, 0x06, 0x07, 0x08}
            }
        }
    linUserConfig.filterCount = 1;
    linUserConfig.slaveFilterCfgPtr = idFilterConfig;
~~~~~
12. This is a code example to set up a user LINFlexD Driver configuration instantiation:
~~~~~{.c}
    /* Device instance number as UART instance*/
    #define LI0 (0U)

    linflexd_state_t linState;
    linflexd_user_config_t linUserConfig;
    /* Set baudrate 19200 bps */
    linUserConfig.baudRate = 19200U;
    /* Node is MASTER */
    linUserConfig.nodeFunction = MASTER;
    /* Disable autobaud feature */
    linUserConfig.autobaudEnable = FALSE;

    /* Initialize LINFlexD Hardware interface */
    LINFlexD_DRV_Init(LI0, (linflexd_user_config_t *) &linUserConfig, (linflexd_state_t *) &linState);
~~~~~

# LINFlexD Data Transfers {#LINDRIVERTrnsf}

The driver implements transmit and receive functions to transfer buffers of data by blocking and non-blocking modes.

The blocking transmit and receive functions include LINFlexD_DRV_MasterTransferBlocking() function.

The non-blocking (async) transmit and receive functions include the LINFlexD_DRV_MasterTransfer() and the LINFlexD_DRV_SlaveResponse() functions.

The LINFlexD_DRV_SlaveResponse() function is recommended to be called in an interrupt event of receiving PID as implemented in LINFlexD Stack middleware.

The LINFlexD_DRV_SlaveResponse() function should be called before data is transferring on the LINFlexD bus.

Master nodes can transmit frame headers in non-blocking mode using LINFlexD_DRV_MasterTransferBlocking().

In all these cases, the functions are interrupt-driven.

# Autobaud feature {#LINDRIVERAutobaud}

AUTOBAUD is an extensive feature in LINFlexD Driver which allows a slave node to automatically detect baudrate of LINFlexD bus
and adapt its original baudrate to bus value. Auto Baud is applied when the baudrate of the incoming data is unknown.
Currently autobaud feature is supported to detect LINFlexD bus baudrates 2400, 4800, 9600, 14400, 19200 bps.

1. If autobaud feature is enabled, at LIN driver initialization slave's baudrate is set to 19200bps. 
When receiving a frame header, the slave detect LIN bus's baudrate based on the synchronization byte and adapts its
baudrate accordingly.

2. Baudrate evaluation process is executed until autobaud successfully. During run-time if LIN bus's baudrate is
changed suddenly to a value other than the slave's current baudrate, users shall reset MCU to execute baudrate
evaluation process.

## Note

1. When the vector table is not in ram (__flash_vector_table__ = 1):
    - INT_SYS_InstallHandler shall check if the function pointer provided as parameter for the new handler is already
    present in the vector table for the given IRQ number.
    - The user will be required to manually add the correct handlers in the startup files

*/